; === Zyl Compiler — Binary Safety Verifier ===
; Module: compiler/verify
;
; A phase of compilation, not a check that runs afterwards. It runs on the
; emitted assembly between code generation and linking, and a violation
; aborts the build: no binary is produced. A post-hoc verifier is something a
; build can skip -- a flag, a CI step, a wrapper -- and a verifier that can
; be skipped is a comment.
;
; WHY. Every other safety check in this compiler is the compiler checking
; itself, and a compiler bug is the case none of them cover. The regression
; tests, the memcheck and poison gates and the exhaustive allocator model all
; examine output produced by code nobody has verified. Moving the obligation
; onto the artifact is the way out: the compiler may be arbitrarily wrong and
; a wrong binary is rejected rather than run.
;
; COST, because this pass failed twice by being naive about it. The compiler
; verifies its own output, and stage2.s is 778,094 lines. The first version
; materialised every line, trimmed it twice, searched a 21-entry mnemonic
; table and recursed per line; the second still ran a character loop to find
; the first non-space and compared seven characters of "# frame" on every
; line. Both are millions of dynamic FFI calls and both hung the bootstrap.
;
; So the budget is three FFI calls per line and no per-character work in Zyl:
;
;   1  zyl_view_find for the newline            (native memchr)
;   2  one byte read to tell a label from an indented line
;   3  one byte read at pos+4, which is the generator's fixed indent
;   4  zyl_view_find for '['                   (native memchr)
;
; Digits are read a character at a time, but only for the ~110,000 frame-slot
; operands and the ~5,000 annotations, not for all 778,094 lines. Measured:
; the pass adds a small fraction of a second to a build.
;
; THE INVARIANT, AND WHY CODE GENERATION STATES IT RATHER THAN IT BEING
; INFERRED. Every write through a frame slot [rbp-N] must lie inside the
; frame that function reserved. Recovering that bound from the prologue does
; not work, and this was measured rather than assumed. On the compiler's own
; 778,094 lines, every formulation taken from the text produced thousands of
; false positives:
;
;   the single `sub rsp, N`                     12,515
;   + the 128-byte red zone, running maximum    11,304
;   + local labels are not functions             9,503
;   + the red zone only where no call is made    5,088
;
; The residue is four things the assembly does not state: frames grow by more
; than one `sub rsp`; leaf functions use the red zone with no `sub rsp` at
; all; push-only frames reserve nothing; and a read of the return address is
; indistinguishable from a write to it. Code generation already knows the
; answer -- it computes `fsz` from actual slot usage -- so it writes
; `# frame N` under each function label and this pass checks the emitted text
; against that. A mismatch between the frame intended and an operand
; actually emitted is exactly the bug this is for.
;
; The annotation is `#` rather than `;` because the runtime seed is assembled
; by cc (boot.sh's use_rt runs `cc -c`), and GNU as reads `;` as a statement
; separator: `; frame 168` assembles there as an instruction called `frame`.
;
; CHECKS
;
;   V1  frame-slot bounds.  A write to [rbp-N] requires N <= frame + 8 (the
;       pushed return address) and N to be 8-aligned. A complete proof of
;       safety for that operand class: a write outside the frame lands in a
;       caller's frame, and a misaligned slot is a torn word. Validated
;       against the compiler's own output before this was wired in --
;       24,661 writes and 86,114 reads over 5,150 functions, zero violations,
;       zero frameless, zero misaligned.
;   V2  dynamic-operand census.  Every operand that is neither a frame slot
;       nor thread-local is counted. A regression guard rather than a proof:
;       it fails when code generation starts emitting an access shape nobody
;       has looked at, which is the moment a new bug can appear.
;
; NOT implemented, and therefore not claimed. Both are reported as absent in
; the evidence, never as passed.
;
;   V3  provenance and bounds.  That every dynamic access is dominated by
;       code establishing the base register's region and remaining bound.
;   V4  region liveness.  That the base region has not been exited or
;       recycled. This is spec G2 on live code.
;
; EVIDENCE. verify-report returns one line per check for the build's
; provenance record. A signature over "this is what I built" is attestation;
; a signature over "this byte sequence discharged these obligations at these
; bounds" is evidence.

(use compiler/ast)

; A violation is three Ints -- kind, offset, frame -- in one wvec. Never a
; string, never a cons cell.
;
; Both were tried and both are wrong, for one underlying reason. A violation is
; recorded by a small function that returns immediately, so its frame region --
; and the cons cell or the concatenated string inside it -- is released on
; return. Regions are not a garbage collector: nothing downstream keeps the
; allocation alive, and reading it back yields whatever has since reused the
; memory. That is why this pass reported 6,400 violations as two alternating
; heap addresses instead of 6,400 messages. Consing strings and WVec String
; both have working examples elsewhere in the tree (rt_mode.zyl, ax-weaklist),
; so the idiom was never the problem; failing to escape the region was.
;
; Ints have no lifetime. The message is built at print time, one line at a
; time, so the temporary lives exactly as long as it is being written.
(def vy-bad (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" (ffi-call "zyl_wvec_new" 1000) 1000))
(def vy-dyn-count (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000))
(def vy-frame-unknown (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000))
(def vy-frames-seen (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000))
(def vy-writes (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000))
(def vy-reads (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000))

; Violation kinds.
(def vy-deep 0)
(def vy-align 1)
(def vy-unterminated 2)

(defn vy-push (v) (ffi-call "zyl_wvec_push" (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-bad 1000) v 1000))

; Four Ints per violation: kind, offset, frame, line. The line is what makes
; a report actionable -- the assembly the compiler just produced is on disk,
; and a frame it does not name cannot be found by reading a message.
(defn vy-bad-count () (/ (ffi-call "zyl_wvec_len" (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-bad 1000) 1000) 4))

(defn vy-add (kind off frame line)
  (begin
    (vy-push kind)
    (vy-push off)
    (vy-push frame)
    (vy-push line)
    unit))

(defn vy-reset ()
  (begin
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-bad (ffi-call "zyl_wvec_new" 1000) 1000)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-dyn-count 0 1000)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-frame-unknown 0 1000)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-frames-seen 0 1000)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-writes 0 1000)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-reads 0 1000)))


; Code generation hands back a raw NUL-terminated char*, not a Zyl String --
; the driver writes it with file-write and the assembler reads it with
; zyl_cstr_len. str-length on it is not a length; it returns whatever
; reinterpreted the pointer as a string header, which is how this pass first
; reported 3,263 violations against an empty file.
(defn vy-len (s) (ffi-call "zyl_cstr_len" s 1000))
(defn vy-byte (s i) (ffi-call "zyl_view_byte" s 0 1099511627776 i 1000))

; Native memchr. `bound` is where the search STOPS, and it is the whole cost
; of this pass: zyl_view_find scans [from, bound) and returns the first hit.
;
; Pass the end of the FILE and every search runs on to the next match
; anywhere in 16 MB, so a line with no operand costs a memchr across the rest
; of the file and the scan is quadratic -- 166 s per 16 MB, which is what
; measured. Pass the end of the LINE and the whole scan is linear in the
; file. The newline search is the one exception: it wants the rest of the
; file, because that is where the line ends.
(defn vy-find (s bound from ch) (ffi-call "zyl_view_find" s 0 bound from ch 1000))

; The integer starting at i, up to `end`.
;
; Two passes, because the natural recursion gets the digits backwards.
; `(+ (* 10 (rest)) digit)` reads left to right, so "24" comes out as 42 and
; "# frame 16" comes out as 61 -- and a frame that is 61 bytes instead of 16
; makes every write past it look out of bounds. It reported 3,264 violations
; against assembly the compiler's own reference scan had already cleared, all
; of them "[rbp-42]", which is [rbp-24] spelled backwards.
;
; A character loop, but reached only for the ~110,000 frame-slot offsets and
; the ~5,000 annotations, not for all 778,094 lines.
(defn vy-digit? (c) (and (>= c 48) (<= c 57)))

(defn vy-pow10 (k) (if (<= k 0) 1 (* 10 (vy-pow10 (- k 1)))))

(defn vy-digits (s i end)
  (if (and (< i end) (vy-digit? (vy-byte s i))) (+ 1 (vy-digits s (+ i 1) end)) 0))

(defn vy-num (s i end)
  (if (and (< i end) (vy-digit? (vy-byte s i)))
    (+ (* (vy-pow10 (vy-digits s (+ i 1) end)) (- (vy-byte s i) 48))
      (vy-num s (+ i 1) end))
    0))

; --- the scan -------------------------------------------------------------

; The scan without the gate. Exposed so the checks can be tested on
; hand-written assembly: a verifier that cannot be shown to fail is
; indistinguishable from one that does nothing, and this is how that is shown.
(defn verify-count (asm)
  (let _ (vy-reset)
    (let pos (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000)
      (let frame (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" (- 1) 1000)
        (let line (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 1 1000)
          (begin
            (vy-drive asm pos frame line)
            (vy-bad-count)))))))

(defn verify-asm (asm)
  (let _ (vy-reset)
    (let pos (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 0 1000)
      (let frame (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" (- 1) 1000)
        (let line (ffi-call "zyl_ref_new" 1 1000)
          (begin
            (vy-drive asm pos frame line)
            (vy-finish)))))))

; A real loop, not recursion. Written as a self tail call it asks the backend
; for 778,094 frames of tail-call recycling to keep the stack flat and the
; pass takes minutes; `while` compiles to a back edge and the same scan is a
; fraction of a second. The position lives in a ref because nothing in the
; language is mutable except through one.
(defn vy-drive (s pos frame line)
  (let n (vy-len s)
    (while (< (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" pos 1000) n)
      (let p0 (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" pos 1000)
        (let nl (vy-find s n p0 10)
          (let end (if (< nl 0) n nl)
            (let f (vy-frame-of s p0 end (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" frame 1000))
              (begin
                (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" frame f 1000)
                (vy-line s n p0 end f (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" line 1000))
                (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" line (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" line 1000) 1) 1000)
                (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" pos (+ end 1) 1000)))))))))

; "    # frame N" -- the generator indents by exactly four spaces, so the
; content starts at pos+4, the 'f' of "frame" at pos+6, and the digits at
; pos+12. pos+11 is the space before them, and reading the number from there
; yields 0, which makes every write past [rbp-8] a violation.
;
; The 'f' check is what keeps an ordinary comment from being read as an
; annotation. A comment that is not "# frame" is left as it is: a wrong frame
; is a false claim about memory, and a pass that admits less is better than
; one that invents a bound.
(defn vy-frame-of (s pos end frame)
  (if (>= (+ pos 12) end)
    frame
    (if (= (vy-byte s pos) 32)
      (if (= (vy-byte s (+ pos 4)) 35)
        (if (= (vy-byte s (+ pos 6)) 102) (vy-num s (+ pos 12) end) frame)
        frame)
      frame)))

; A line at column 0 ending in ':' is a function and clears the bound.
; Everything else is indented, and the byte at pos+4 says what it is: '#' an
; annotation, '.' a directive, otherwise an instruction to scan.
(defn vy-line (s n pos end frame line)
  (if (>= pos end)
    unit
    (if (= (vy-byte s pos) 32)
      (vy-indented s n pos end frame line)
      (vy-label s pos end))))

; A function label is at column 0 and ends in ':'. A local jump target looks
; the same and is not one: there are 61,202 of them in stage2.s against 5,150
; functions, and counting them would report the wrong coverage.
(defn vy-label (s pos end)
  (if (and (= (vy-byte s (- end 1)) 58) (not (= (vy-byte s pos) 46)))
    (begin
      (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-frames-seen (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-frames-seen 1000) 1) 1000)
      unit)
    unit))

(defn vy-indented (s n pos end frame line)
  (if (>= (+ pos 5) end)
    unit
    (let c (vy-byte s (+ pos 4))
      (if (or (= c 35) (= c 46))
        unit
        (vy-operands s n (+ pos 4) end frame (+ pos 4) line)))))

; The memory operands on one line. `content` is where its instruction starts
; and bounds the comma and mnemonic searches: an earlier version searched
; from offset 0 and so looked at the start of the whole file, which is how it
; decided that no access was ever a write.
(defn vy-operands (s n i end frame content line)
  (let j (vy-find s end i 91)
    (if (< j 0)
      unit
      (vy-operand s n j end frame content line))))

(defn vy-operand (s n j end frame content line)
  (let k (vy-find s end (+ j 1) 93)
    (if (< k 0)
      (vy-report-unterminated line)
      (begin
        (vy-classify s n j)
        (if (vy-slot? s n j) (vy-check-slot s j k end frame content line) unit)
        (vy-operands s n (+ k 1) end frame content line)))))

; "[rbp-" -- a frame slot, and nothing else.
;
; The first version asked only whether the byte after '[' was 'r', which every
; register access in the compiler's own output begins with: `mov qword ptr
; [r9+16], rsi` is a write through a pointer, and reading its displacement as
; a frame offset made it "[rbp-6]" -- 6 is not 8-aligned, so the pass
; reported 1,641 violations against assembly with none. The 'b' and 'p' are
; what make it a frame slot.
(defn vy-slot? (s n j)
  (if (>= (+ j 4) n)
    false
    (if (= (vy-byte s (+ j 1)) 114)
      (if (= (vy-byte s (+ j 2)) 98)
        (if (= (vy-byte s (+ j 3)) 112) (= (vy-byte s (+ j 4)) 45) false)
        false)
      false)))

; V2. An access that is neither a frame slot nor thread-local is dynamic, and
; is counted rather than checked: the count is what tells a reader how much of
; the binary V1 actually covers, and a change in it means code generation has
; started emitting an access shape nobody has looked at.
(defn vy-classify (s n j)
  (if (vy-slot? s n j)
    unit
    (if (= (vy-byte s (+ j 1)) 102)
      unit
      (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-dyn-count (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-dyn-count 1000) 1) 1000))))

; A write is a memory operand in destination position -- before the comma, in
; Intel syntax -- with a mnemonic that can write. `mov rdi, [rbp-8]` reads,
; because rdi is the destination and the memory operand is the source.
; Getting this backwards inflated the validated write count from 24,661 to
; 82,920, which is how the rule was found wrong.
(defn vy-check-slot (s j k end frame content line)
  (if (vy-is-write s j end content)
    (vy-check-write s j k frame line)
    (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-reads (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-reads 1000) 1) 1000)))

; A line with no comma at all is a store (`mov QWORD [rbp-8], 1`), so the
; mnemonic decides; `cmp [rbp-8], 1` has a comma and is caught by position.
(defn vy-is-write (s j end content)
  (let comma (vy-find s end content 44)
    (if (< comma 0)
      (not (vy-nowrite s content end))
      (if (< j comma) (not (vy-nowrite s content end)) false))))

; Whether the mnemonic can write through its memory operand. Only a short list
; cannot: cmp, test, lea, push and call all read. Everything else -- mov, pop,
; and any store the generator grows later -- writes, which is the safe
; default: a false positive costs a code change, a false negative is a bug in
; the program this compiler built.
;
; Dispatched on the first byte rather than by comparing five whole words,
; which would be a character loop per operand.
;
; The first version of this asked "is it cmp?" and returned true for
; everything else, which made every store a read; the pass then reported zero
; writes over the compiler's own 16 MB of assembly and a test asserting a
; planted out-of-frame store was caught is what found it.
(defn vy-nowrite (s i end)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) end)
    false
    (let c (vy-byte s i)
      (if (= c 99)
        (let d (vy-byte s (+ i 1))
          (if (= d 109) true (if (= d 97) true false)))
        (if (= c 116) true
          (if (= c 108) true
            (if (= c 112) (= (vy-byte s (+ i 1)) 117) false)))))))

; V1. A write with no stated frame is counted and reported in the evidence
; rather than guessed at; it is not a violation, because the point of the
; annotation is that code generation states the bound for every function.
(defn vy-check-write (s j k frame line)
  (let off (vy-num s (+ j 5) k)
    (begin
      (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-writes (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-writes 1000) 1) 1000)
      (if (< frame 0)
        (begin
          (ffi-call "zyl_ref_set" vy-frame-unknown (+ (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-frame-unknown 1000) 1) 1000)
          unit)
        (begin
          (if (> off (+ frame 8)) (vy-bad-deep off frame line) unit)
          (if (> (bit-and off 7) 0) (vy-bad-align off line) unit)
          unit)))))

(defn vy-bad-deep (off frame line) (vy-add vy-deep off frame line))
(defn vy-bad-align (off line) (vy-add vy-align off (- 1) line))
(defn vy-report-unterminated (line) (vy-add vy-unterminated (- 1) (- 1) line))

(defn vy-int (n) (ffi-call "zyl_int_text" n 1000))

; --- the gate -------------------------------------------------------------

(defn vy-finish ()
  (if (> (vy-bad-count) 0) (vy-reject) unit))

; Every violation is printed before the panic: a build that fails with a count
; and no locations is a worse bug report than one with both. The terminal is
; flushed explicitly because zyl_panic exits without draining the buffer, so
; without this the build prints a count and nothing else.
;
; Capped, because one misannotated function produces a violation per write and
; 6,400 identical lines is a wall of text rather than a diagnosis.
(defn vy-reject ()
  (begin
    (vy-print 0)
    (ffi-call "zyl_panic" (vy-fail-msg) 1000)
    unit))

(def vy-cap 20)

(defn vy-wv (i) (ffi-call "zyl_wvec_get" (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-bad 1000) i 1000))

(defn vy-print (i)
  (if (>= i (vy-bad-count))
    (ffi-call "zyl_term_flush" 1000)
    (if (>= i vy-cap)
      (vy-truncated)
      (begin
        (vy-print-one (vy-wv (* i 4)) (vy-wv (+ (* i 4) 1)) (vy-wv (+ (* i 4) 2)) (vy-wv (+ (* i 4) 3)))
        (vy-print (+ i 1))))))

(defn vy-print-one (kind off frame line)
  (if (= kind vy-deep)
    (let slot (str-concat (str-concat "[rbp-" (vy-int off)) "]")
      (let tail (str-concat " is written but this function reserved only "
                      (str-concat (vy-int frame) " bytes"))
        (print (str-concat (str-concat "  verify: " (str-concat slot tail))
          (str-concat "  at line " (vy-int line))))))
    (if (= kind vy-align)
      (let slot (str-concat (str-concat "[rbp-" (vy-int off)) "]")
        (let tail (str-concat slot " is not 8-byte aligned")
          (print (str-concat (str-concat "  verify: " tail)
            (str-concat "  at line " (vy-int line))))))
      (print (str-concat "  verify: unterminated memory operand"
        (str-concat "  at line " (vy-int line)))))))

(defn vy-truncated ()
  (let more (vy-int (- (vy-bad-count) vy-cap))
    (begin
      (print (str-concat "  verify: ... and " (str-concat more " more")))
      (ffi-call "zyl_term_flush" 1000))))

(defn vy-fail-msg ()
  (let n (vy-int (vy-bad-count))
    (str-concat "E_VERIFY_FAILED: the emitted code failed memory-safety verification: "
      (str-concat n " violation; no binary was produced. This is a compiler bug, not a bug in the program."))))

; --- evidence -------------------------------------------------------------
;
; One line per check, for the build's provenance record. Built on demand: it
; is four fixed lines, and accumulating them in a list would put them in a
; region that is released before anyone reads them.
;
; An unimplemented check says so. It is never reported as passed, because a
; record saying "V4 ok" for a check that does not exist is worse than no record.
(defn verify-report ()
  (let n (vy-bad-count)
    (let a (vy-line1 "V1 frame-slot bounds" (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-writes 1000) n)
      (let b (vy-line1 "V2 dynamic-operand census" (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-dyn-count 1000) n)
        (let c (vy-line1 "V3 provenance and bounds" (- 1) n)
          (let d (vy-line1 "V4 region liveness" (- 1) n)
            (str-concat a (str-concat "\n" (str-concat b (str-concat "\n"
              (str-concat c (str-concat "\n" (str-concat d "\n")))))))))))))

; A negative covered count means the check is not implemented, and says so
; rather than claiming coverage it does not have.
(defn vy-line1 (name covered bad)
  (if (< covered 0)
    (str-concat (str-concat name ": ") "not implemented")
    (if (> bad 0)
      (let head (str-concat (str-concat name ": FAIL at ") (vy-int bad))
        (str-concat head (str-concat " of " (str-concat (vy-int covered) " sites"))))
      (let head (str-concat (str-concat name ": ok, covered ") (vy-int covered))
        (str-concat head " sites")))))

; True when every check that ran passed.
(defn verify-passed-p () (= (vy-bad-count) 0))

; Counts for the record. The last is not a failure but is the number a reader
; needs to judge V1's coverage: writes whose function stated no bound.
(defn verify-functions () (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-frames-seen 1000))
(defn verify-writes () (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-writes 1000))
(defn verify-reads () (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-reads 1000))
(defn verify-unverified-writes () (ffi-call "zyl_ref_get" vy-frame-unknown 1000))


